>> title: Overlord Flower Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, overlord flower, apricot kernels, cantonese, old-fire soup, autumn, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 霸王花南北杏豬骨湯, baa wong faa naam bak hang zyu gwat tong, ba wong fa nam bak hang jue gwat tong, overlord flower soup, dried night-blooming cactus flower soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 45 min
>> slack: forgiving — dried cactus flower holds grit in its folds, so skipping the soak puts grit in the bowl that no straining lifts
>> step.1: Autumn. The flower is almost tasteless cooked — it is in the pot for phlegm and a faint sweetness behind it.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: soak the flower 30 min, then rinse it hard
>> step.4: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids are spent.

Autumn. The flower is unmistakable dried and almost tasteless cooked — it is in the pot for phlegm and for the faint sweetness it leaves behind, which is the tradition's own reason for it and the only one there is.

Blanch @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every bone under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Soak @dried overlord flower{2%oz}(60 g; baa wong faa, dried night-blooming cactus flower) in cold water, ~soak{30%min}, then open every fold under the tap and rinse it until the water runs clear, and rinse @apricot kernels{1/4%cup}(naam bak hang; sweet and bitter together, about three to one — the bitter kind goes in small and always cooked through, never raw), @honey dates{2}(mat zou; the sweetening, since there is no sugar in the pot) and @dried figs{2}(mou faa gwo; drier sweetness than the date) with it.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched bones{} with @&(~1)soaked flower and dried goods{} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
